<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guo (state)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1046–655 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guo was the name of several states of Zhou-period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ruled by kinsmen of the Zhou royal house, descended from younger brothers of King Wen. Eastern Guo, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingyang">Xingyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, was destroyed by the State of Zheng around 767 BCE, while Western Guo, later based at Shangyang near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanmenxia">Sanmenxia</a>, was conquered by Jin in 655 BCE after Jin marched through the territory of Yu. Its dukes served as senior ministers at the Zhou court.</p></div>
